   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.4. Increased capacity of constituents to provide adequate labour protection to workers in diverse forms of work arrangements, including on digital labour platforms, and in informal employment</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Research Institute on Labour and Social Protection (RILSP) of the Ministry of Labour and Social Protection (MLSP) has conducted an  informal employment study in 2021, which includes a rapid assessment of the consequences of COVID-19 for workers in informal employment. The study shed light on   (i) adverse working conditions of informal workers, (ii) challenges and opportunities faced by both workers and economic units in transitioning  to the formal economy, and (iii) pandemic&#x2019;s impact; and included (iv) policy recommendations to support formalization and address challenges and opportunities of those in informal employment in recovery measures. The study identifies subgroups that face opportunities for formalization given proper incentives and those with specific vulnerabilities and disadvantages. 
The study findings have been validated with the national stakeholders including social partners and released in the fourth quarter of the year. The Study will feed into development of a state policy on informal economy. It was also a part of UN-SERP Mongolia. Moreover, the methodology that was introduced with the study will be used in future studies, thus, improved the research capacity of the institution. The ILO provided technical guidance on the choice of the method, the formulation of questions, the analysis of data and review of the report. The ILO also ensured a participatory approach throughout the process, ensuring a leading role by the labour ministry, organizing stakeholder meetings including social partners to discuss the implementation of the study and the results.</narrative>
   </description>
